WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.660
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.080
Online video has always been about relatable, authentic, even independent content.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.100
That's the fascinating part, right? That anybody could do this.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.400
But, can they?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.480
It has gotten much harder, and the perception of it has gotten much more mainstream.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.660
To be both authentic and relatable becomes very hard when you're doing this professionally.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.600
There's not much less relatable than being the CEO of a media company, and I'm the CEO of a media company

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.880
now. I don't really know how that happened.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:30.740
With VidCon and SciShow and Crash Course and DFTBA Records and all of our other productions,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.460
we have like thirty employees now.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.440
Now this is still solidly a small business. Like, we have fewer employees than the average McDonald's.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.560
But I've had a lot of problems in the last year that are super unrelatable that I'm not going to talk about

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.740
in videos. Nobody wants to hear me, like, whine about how complicated it is to figure out how to properly

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.020
classify employment insurance for Crash Course freelancers.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:57.020
I love YouTube. I love all of YouTube, but I really love the independent weird stuff where someone finds a niche

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.700
or a format that's just perfect for their skills and their style.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.120
The Super Carlin Brothers, after years of making content for a very small audience, recently found a format

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.740
that has become hugely successful.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:13.720
And Bill Wurtz's History of Japan is a weird art piece from a creator that came out of nowhere

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.260
that I think everybody should watch.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:20.480
But when we started, there was a lot of attention and next to no content, so it was really easy to get noticed.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.000
Now, it could hardly be more crowded.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.880
And the number one thing you need to get attention is attention and most of the people in companies

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.140
who have attention want to turn that attention back on themselves or to sell it to advertisers.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.580
If you're making great content, but nobody's seeing it, it's harder and harder to get a leg up.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.440
We do some of that with things like The Financial Diet, and Healthcare Triage and Sexplanations

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.080
and Animal Wonders.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.640
And of course we've got the Vlogbrothers ad revenue grant. This month we sent money to Science With Tom

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.720
Eternally Curious, SimonOxfPhys and Unacademy, which is India's largest free education initiative.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.420
But it's always easiest to turn attention and money to new things that we are doing, like NerdCon

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.860
or Dear Hank and John, or SciShow and Crash Course.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.600
And of course, we love those things. We think they're good for the world and we love doing them

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.200
and that they're things that we do with this community, but they are businesses in a-

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.880
They're more traditional productions, you know?

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.740
Like, there's a management structure and people work together to create something rather than,

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:16.520
like, Vlogbrothers, which is just I write some stuff and then I talk some stuff and then I edit some stuff and

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.500
upload some stuff.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.440
That seems like the spirit of YouTube to me and it seems maybe a little bit unfair that someone like me

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:29.640
can use the money and attention that I have to launch new projects and to bring people together,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.820
talented, awesome people, to help me create new things,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.280
when the vast majority of people don't have access to those resources, so they can't compete with that.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.660
The pure meritocracy that's maybe part of the mythology of YouTube is long gone,

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:48.440
and one has to wonder whether that is one, creating frustration and two, decreasing the number of people

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.860
who are taking chances to do weird, amazing, wonderful things on this platform.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.500
Now, nobody really knows what YouTube is, but I think that the best analogy, at least for the creative

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.060
personality driven parts, is the music industry.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:06.400
You're going to have at the top, like, a rotating cast of really well known people and a few different

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.540
really big genres.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.980
They're going to make great content, it's going to be polished and appealing and lots of people are going to

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.920
love it, but it's going to be, maybe, a tiny bit bland.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:19.140
And then there will be cascading levels down of people who have smaller but more dedicated audiences

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.800
who perform in sub-genres or genres you might have never heard of just outside of any genre.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.940
And they'll be doing the really creative, weird interesting stuff that will sometimes, I'm sure, bubble up

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:31.480
to the top and affect everything.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.700
Usually they won't, and that's why I think it's so important that we use tools like Patreon to support

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:36.720
creators like that.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.220
For you and me, John, I just think it's important that

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.040
even though not everything in our lives has remained normal

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.360
and super relatable and we have pretty weird lives now,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.060
that we nonetheless remain authentic and maybe we lose a little bit of our relatabililty because of that,

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.860
but that's reality.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.780
I want to be open with the people of this community and this video is part of that.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.402
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

